#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# join

#% text
# `join(xs, ys, ...)` gives one list of the elements of the lists `xs`, `ys`, ... in order.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# Any number of lists may be joined; `join(xs)` is `xs`.

#% item
# Only one level is joined: elements that are lists stay lists.

#% item
# The arguments must all have the same head; joining terms with another head (`join(f(a), f(b))`) gives `f(a, b)`.

#% item
# For a list of lists, `catenate(xss)` joins them all.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
join([1, 2], [3, 4])
#% output
#= [1, 2, 3, 4]

#% cell
join([a], [], [b, c], [d])
#% output
#= [a, b, c, d]

#% cell
join([[1, 2]], [[3, 4]])
#% output
#= [[1, 2], [3, 4]]

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# Terms with another head:

#% cell
join(f(a, b), f(c))
#% output
#= f(a, b, c)

#% subsection
# Applications

#% text
# The x values of a fine grid near 0 joined to a coarse grid further out:

#% cell
join(range(0, 1, 1/4), range(2, 4))
#% output
#= [0, 1/4, 1/2, 3/4, 1, 2, 3, 4]

#% text
# Data from several runs combined before a summary:

#% cell
run1 = [1.2, 0.9, 1.1]
run2 = [1.0, 1.3]
mean(join(run1, run2))
#% output
#= 1.1

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# Lengths add:

#% cell
length(join(range(3), range(5)))
#% output
#= 8

#% text
# `catenate` joins a list of lists; `append` adds a single element:

#% cell
catenate([[1, 2], [3], [4, 5]])
#% output
#= [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]

#% cell
[join([1, 2], [3]), append([1, 2], [3])]
#% output
#= [[1, 2, 3], [1, 2, [3]]]

#% subsection
# Possible issues

#% text
# A list and a non-list cannot be joined:

#% cell
join([1, 2], 3)
#% output
#! failure(argument, join, 2)

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `catenate`, `append`, `prepend`, `flatten`, `union`
